201 Created - Returns TeamReadDto with the newly created team and its assigned ID
Example: { "teamId": 1, "name": "Manchester United", "coach": "Erik ten Hag", "foundedYear": 1878 }
Location header points to GET /api/teams/1

member.summary:
Navigation property: Reference to the Team this player belongs to

Why [JsonIgnore]?
Without it, when serializing a Player to JSON, the Team object would be included.
But Team has a Players collection, which includes this Player, creating a circular reference:

Without [JsonIgnore] (INFINITE LOOP):
GET /api/players/1 → 
{
  "PlayerId": 1,
  "FullName": "John",
  "Team": {
    "TeamId": 5,
    "Name": "Arsenal",
    "Players": [
      {
        "PlayerId": 1,
        "FullName": "John",
        "Team": {
          "TeamId": 5,
          "Players": [ ... INFINITE! ]
        }
      }
    ]
  }
}

With [JsonIgnore] (CLEAN):
GET /api/players/1 → 
{
  "PlayerId": 1,
  "FullName": "John",
  "TeamId": 5
  // Team property not included in JSON
}

Client knows TeamId = 5, and can fetch the team separately if needed:
GET /api/teams/5

This pattern is called "foreign key exposure" - don't expose collections,
just expose the ID, and let client fetch related data if needed (REST principle).

Constructor - Dependency Injection
DI Container (in Program.cs) provides ITeamRepository and ILogger instances

Example of dependency injection in action:
var service = new TeamService(repository, logger);
// DI container creates these for you:
var repository = new TeamRepository(dbContext);
var logger = loggerFactory.CreateLogger<TeamService>();
